NAME
    sh - run shell commands from a script file

SYNOPSIS
    sh file

DESCRIPTION
    Reads `file` (a text file of shell command lines, up to 512 bytes) and
    runs each line exactly as if you had typed it at the prompt: built-ins,
    /bin programs, and redirection/pipes (>, <, |) all work. Lines are echoed
    as they run. At end of file, control returns to the interactive prompt.

    sh is a shell built-in, not a /bin program. The whole script is read into
    memory first, so a command's own file I/O can't disturb it -- with one
    exception: a script line may not use the `>>` append redirect (it shares
    the script's buffer). Lines end with CR, LF, or CRLF.

    The main use is rebuilding a command from source on the machine. A build
    script for a C command:

        cc /src/commands/c/pwd.c >T.ASM
        asm T.ASM /bin/pwd.bin

    and for a hand-assembled command (asm resolves ;#use from /lib):

        asm /src/commands/asm/cat.asm /bin/cat.bin

EXAMPLES
    sh build            run the commands in the file `build`
    sh /mkpwd           rebuild pwd from /src and reinstall it

SEE ALSO
    cc, asm, run, edit
